# Every text file is stored with LF in the repository. Without this rule a
# Windows checkout rewrites files to CRLF on disk and git then reports them as
# modified forever: `git status` filled up with three dozen documents, READMEs
# and even dev certificates that nobody had opened, and real changes had to be
# picked out of that noise by hand before every commit.
#
# `text=auto` still leaves genuinely binary files alone — git decides by
# content, not by extension. Working copies keep whatever endings the platform
# checks out; only what is committed is normalised.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The rules below are implied by the line above and kept for one reason: they
# say WHY these particular files must never be CRLF, which the blanket rule
# cannot.
#
# Windows checkouts turn LF into CRLF, and a shell script with CRLF fails in a
# Linux container in the most confusing way available: the kernel reads the
# shebang as "/bin/sh\r", cannot find that interpreter, and the entrypoint
# reports "not found" — about the file, which plainly exists.
#
# Anything executed inside a container stays LF regardless of the platform.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh   text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
